course: medicine
lesson: 61
title: The World Cup
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: The men's World Cup comes round every four years, and in 2026 it grew from 32 teams to 48 and was shared by three hosts, the USA, Canada and Mexico, so there were 104 matches instead of 64.
explain: Callum from Gateshead stares at the wallchart, «Howay, 48 teams? That's proper mental, man» (Geordie for come on, that's crazy), and the sums work out: 12 groups of 4, each group playing 6 games, then 32 teams in the knockouts.
explain: Watching is exercise for your heart too: a 3–3 final with penalties makes your pulse race and your hands sweat on the sofa, and a 2 am kick-off from Los Angeles eats the sleep a growing body needs.
explain: Fans have bodies as well as players: three hours of shouting can leave your voice «banjaxed» (Irish for broken), three hours in July sun can bring on heat exhaustion, and the thin air of Mexico City's Azteca leaves visitors puffing.
fact: The 2026 men's World Cup was hosted by the USA, Canada and Mexico, with 48 teams and 104 matches, up from 32 teams and 64 matches.
fact: In 2026 the 48 teams were split into 12 groups of 4; each group played 6 games, 72 group games in all, and 32 teams went through to the knockouts.
fact: Japan and South Korea shared the 2002 World Cup, so 2026 was not the first shared one.
fact: Argentina beat France on penalties after a 3–3 draw in the 2022 final in Qatar.
fact: Most 2026 matches were played in North American time zones, so a 6 pm kick-off in Los Angeles was 2 am in the UK.
fact: Brazil have won the men's World Cup five times, in 1958, 1962, 1970, 1994 and 2002, more than any other country.
fact: England's men won the World Cup once, in 1966, beating West Germany 4–2 after extra time at Wembley.
fact: The 2023 Women's World Cup, hosted by Australia and New Zealand, had 32 teams for the first time; Spain beat England 1–0 in the final, and Australia's Matildas reached the semi-finals.
fact: Shouting for a long time strains the vocal cords so they swell and the voice goes hoarse; resting it and sipping water usually bring it back in a few days.
fact: Sticker packs are random, so doubles keep coming and filling an album takes far more packs than stickers divided by pack size; swapping doubles with friends fills the gaps far more cheaply.
fact: In hot sun the body sweats out water and salt, and fans standing for hours can get heat exhaustion: dizziness, headache and feeling sick; shade, a hat and plenty of water help prevent it.
fact: Estadio Azteca in Mexico City is about 2,200 m above sea level, where each breath brings less oxygen; it hosted World Cup matches in 1970, 1986 and 2026.
fact: «Ripper» is Australian for great, «güey» is Mexican Spanish for dude, and «knackered» is British for exhausted.
exercise: Callum: «48 teams? Three countries hosting?» Who hosted in 2026? | The USA, Canada and Mexico | England, Scotland and Wales | Brazil, Argentina and Chile | Spain, Portugal and Morocco
exercise: How many matches were there at the 2026 World Cup? | 104 | 64 | 48 | 32
exercise: Priya's chart has 12 groups of 4. How many teams is that? | 48 | 16 | 32 | 64
exercise: Each group of 4 plays everyone once. How many games in one group? | 6 | 4 | 8 | 12
exercise: Mateo watches the 2022 final replay. What was the score after extra time? | 3–3, then Argentina won on penalties | 1–0 to France | 2–2, then France won on penalties | 4–2 to Argentina in normal time
exercise: Ollie wants to watch an LA game at 6 pm local time. What time is that in Bristol? | 2 am | 8 pm | 10 pm | 6 pm
exercise: Ravi: «Brazil's won it like ten times». How many really? | 5 | 10 | 3 | 7
exercise: Grandad: «1966, son. Wembley. 4–2». Whom did England beat? | West Germany | Argentina | Brazil | Italy
exercise: Matilda: «The Matildas made the semis at OUR World Cup!» Which year and hosts? | 2023, Australia and New Zealand | 2019, France | 2022, Qatar | 2015, Canada
exercise: Who won the 2023 Women's World Cup final? | Spain, 1–0 against England | England, 1–0 against Spain | Australia, against Sweden | USA, against the Netherlands
exercise: Siobhan's voice is banjaxed after extra time. What happened to her throat? | The vocal cords swelled from strain | Her tonsils fell out | She caught a cold from shouting at the telly | Her lungs got too small
exercise: Aarav's album has 600 stickers, 5 per pack. Why does he need more than 120 packs? | Packs are random, so doubles keep coming | Some packs are always empty | Stickers shrink in the album | 600 ÷ 5 is really 200
exercise: Kayla waits three hours in July sun and everything spins. What is it likely to be? | Heat exhaustion | Too much excitement | A broken ankle | Hay fever
exercise: Diego: «The visitors are always puffing at the Azteca». Why? | High up, each breath has less oxygen | The pitch is extra long | The crowd blows hot air at them all game | Mexican balls are heavier
exercise: How many World Cups has the Azteca hosted matches for? | 3 | 1 | 2 | 5
exercise: Jack says the visitors were «proper knackered». What does he mean? | Very tired | Very happy | Very fast | Very angry
test: Callum's mate: «Only one country can host a World Cup, man». Which fact proves him wrong? | Japan and South Korea shared 2002 | Only Brazil has ever hosted the World Cup | Hosts are always islands, like Japan | No country has ever hosted it twice
test: Priya: «Group games don't matter, it only gets good after». What's true? | Teams can go out in the groups | Every team reaches the knockouts | Group games have no goals | Group results are thrown away
test: Mum to Ollie: «It kicks off at 2 in the morning, bruv. Nice try». Best plan? | Watch the highlights in the morning | Stay up and catch up on sleep in maths class | Catch up on sleep next month | Set an alarm for 1:55 am
test: Ravi's cousin: «Count 'em». Which list is right for Brazil's titles? | 1958, 1962, 1970, 1994, 2002 | 1966, 1970, 1986, 2002, 2014 | 1950, 1958, 1962, 2002, 2010 | Every World Cup since 1930
test: Freddie: «You were three, Grandad». In 2026, how many years since 1966? | 60 | 50 | 66 | 40
test: Mateo can't look at the penalties and his heart pounds. What's happening? | Tension speeds up his heart even sitting down | His heart only beats fast when he runs | He's getting a fever from the telly | Watching sport slows the heart
test: Siobhan whispers all day to save her voice. Better plan? | Rest the voice and sip water | Whisper even more to keep it working | Shout loudly to stretch the cords back | Eat salty crisps to soothe the throat
test: Aarav: «it's giving scam» about his fifth Messi. What's a smart move? | Swap the doubles with friends | Buy 100 more packs at once until it's done | Throw all the doubles away in the bin | Glue two stickers together to make a new one
test: Kayla: «I'm fine, I'm not even playing». Who can get heat exhaustion? | Fans too, not just players | Only the players who run about | Only goalkeepers standing in the sun | Only people out in the cold in winter
test: What would happen if Kayla's family brought hats, shade and water? | She'd be much less likely to feel dizzy | She'd faint faster | She'd need no drinks for a week | Nothing, heat can't be helped
test: Matilda: «Lekker — sorry, that's my Saffa mate's word. Ripper!» What does «ripper» mean? | Great | Ripped shirt | Penalty | Boring
exam: Identify the number of national teams which participated in the men's World Cup of 2026. | Forty-eight | Thirty-two | Sixty-four | Twenty-four
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the record of Brazil in the men's World Cup? | Brazil has secured the title on five occasions | Brazil has secured the title on every occasion | Brazil has never secured the title | Brazil has secured the title on ten occasions
exam: It is given that a fixture commences at six o'clock in the evening in Los Angeles, eight hours behind the United Kingdom. Determine the corresponding time in the United Kingdom. | Two o'clock in the morning | Ten o'clock in the morning | Eight o'clock in the evening | Midnight precisely
exam: Ascertain the principal reason for which visiting players tire more rapidly at the Estadio Azteca. | Each breath at that altitude supplies less oxygen | The pitch is of excessive length | The ball is of greater weight | The spectators impede respiration
